content:
{
  "approved_angle": {
    "title": "Why Carmen wanted more time and another set of eyes on a venue campaign",
    "core_perspective": "We want enough evidence to make a useful recommendation. In office hours, Carmen considered how much traffic a venue campaign had received, looked at what visitors were doing after the click and recommended Jacqueline's feedback on the landing page. She kept the open questions open."
  },
  "sharpened_point": {
    "text": "Giving a campaign more time can go alongside a landing-page review. Expert guidance gives the owner something useful to examine while the campaign gathers more data.",
    "reason": "Makes the practical value of Carmen's judgment and collaboration clear, without turning the piece into another general lesson about tracking."
  },
  "strategic_purpose": {
    "pillar": "WARM",
    "content_type": "behind_the_scenes",
    "why_now": "Show the judgment available in Accelerator office hours after launch: assessing the campaign's opportunity to perform, examining visitor behavior and bringing in the right specialist. The work builds trust without an offer pitch."
  },
  "audience": {
    "who": "Wedding venue owners investing in paid advertising to reach more couples and expand what is already working.",
    "reason_to_care": "They want a useful answer to 'Should we change the campaign yet?' This shows how expert guidance can distinguish what needs more time from what deserves a closer review."
  },
  "intended_shift": {
    "understand": "Gathering more campaign data and reviewing the landing page can happen alongside each other.",
    "believe": "WIA's support includes thoughtful judgment and collaboration, even before the next campaign change is clear.",
    "do": null
  },
  "point_of_view": "we",
  "wia_perspective": "When we recommend giving a campaign more time, we also look at what would be useful to examine along the way. Our Google Ads lead considers how much traffic there is to assess and what visitors do after the click. In her work, visits lasting only a few seconds signal that something's off with the ads. Visitors who spend time exploring and then stop short of an inquiry prompt a closer look at the page. Those observations guide the next question. Bringing in our Meta ads specialist for landing-page feedback gives the owner another way to examine the experience while the campaign gathers more data.",
  "supporting_example": {
    "scene": "In office hours, our Google Ads lead considered the campaign's traffic alongside visitor behavior after the click. She wanted to give the campaign more breathing room and look more closely at the landing page.",
    "wia_move": "She recommended asking our Meta ads specialist for feedback on the page. Her guidance combined more time to assess the campaign with another set of expert eyes on the inquiry experience.",
    "client_contribution": "For the owner, the useful next step is to request feedback on the landing page while continuing to gather campaign data."
  },
  "narrative_outline": {
    "hooks": [
      {
        "style": "question",
        "text": "What can we review while a campaign gathers more data?"
      },
      {
        "style": "observation",
        "text": "In office hours, our Google Ads lead paired more time for a campaign with another set of eyes on the landing page."
      },
      {
        "style": "contrarian_belief",
        "text": "Patience and a closer review can work together."
      }
    ],
    "problems_desires_symptoms": [
      "The owner wants to know what to keep running and what deserves attention.",
      "A campaign needs more traffic to assess, but visitor behavior can still suggest where to look.",
      "The owner values a specific next step rather than a quick verdict."
    ],
    "main_point": "Show how Carmen combined evidence gathering with a recommendation for Jacqueline's landing-page feedback.",
    "transformation": "Move from expecting an immediate campaign verdict to valuing a clear plan for what to examine next.",
    "cta": "No explicit ask. Close on the value of expert judgment."
  },
  "format_plan": {
    "reels": [
      {
        "job": "Show that recommending more time can include a concrete next step. Use one new talking-head script rather than building several clips from the same narrow scene.",
        "cover_text": "While the campaign gathers data",
        "spoken_opening": "What can we review while a campaign gathers more data?",
        "script": "What can we review while a campaign gathers more data?\n\nIn office hours, our Google Ads lead was looking at how much traffic there was to assess and what visitors were doing after they clicked.\n\nThose are different questions, and they help us make a more useful recommendation.\n\nShe wanted to give the campaign more breathing room, and she also recommended getting our Meta ads specialist's feedback on the landing page.\n\nThat's an approach worth paying attention to when you're deciding what to keep running for your venue.\n\nMore time can help us assess how a campaign is performing. A page review gives us another way to examine the experience after the click, including the path to the inquiry form.\n\nWe can use both to guide the next decision.\n\nWe want enough evidence to make a useful recommendation, and we want the owner to understand what we're looking at along the way.",
        "beats": [
          "Open with the question about what can be reviewed while data accumulates.",
          "Introduce the Google Ads lead's two considerations: traffic and behavior after the click.",
          "Pair more breathing room with the recommendation for specialist landing-page feedback.",
          "Connect the approach to a venue owner's decision about what to keep running.",
          "Close on useful recommendations and a clear view of the team's reasoning."
        ],
        "read_time_seconds": 72,
        "source": "new_script"
      }
    ],
    "carousel": {
      "job": "Unpack the reasoning behind the recommendation, with a distinct role for traffic, visitor behavior and specialist feedback.",
      "slides": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "job": "Introduce the perspective and connect it to the venue owner's decision.",
          "text": "Patience and a closer review can work together.\n\nWhen you're investing in ads to bring more couples to your venue, you want to know what to keep running and what deserves another look. A recommendation to gather more data can also include a useful next step."
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "job": "Ground the principle in Carmen's office-hours guidance.",
          "text": "In office hours, our Google Ads lead considered how much traffic there was to assess alongside what visitors were doing after the click.\n\nShe wanted to give the campaign more breathing room. She also recommended getting our Meta ads specialist's feedback on the landing page. Both were part of her guidance."
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "job": "Explain why traffic matters to the timing of a decision.",
          "text": "A campaign needs an opportunity to show us how it's performing.\n\nWe consider how much traffic there is to assess before recommending a change. The number of days since launch doesn't tell us how many people have actually visited the page. That distinction matters when we're deciding whether to keep gathering data."
        },
        {
          "number": 4,
          "job": "Preserve the Google Ads lead's own experience without making an industry-wide claim.",
          "text": "Visitor behavior helps our Google Ads lead decide where to look.\n\nIn her work, visits lasting only a few seconds signal that something's off with the ads. When visitors spend time exploring but stop short of an inquiry, she looks more closely at what happens on the page. Those patterns give her different questions to investigate."
        },
        {
          "number": 5,
          "job": "Show the value of bringing in the right specialist.",
          "text": "Another set of eyes can be a useful next step.\n\nOur Google Ads lead recommended asking our Meta ads specialist for landing-page feedback. That brings another perspective to the experience after the click. The recommendation gives the owner a specific part of the campaign to put forward for review."
        },
        {
          "number": 6,
          "job": "Explain how evidence gathering and page review fit together.",
          "text": "The campaign can gather more traffic while the page gets a closer look.\n\nOne gives us more information about performance. The other helps us examine the experience a visitor encounters before inquiring. Together, they give us more to work with when we consider the next change."
        },
        {
          "number": 7,
          "job": "Close on what this demonstrates about WIA's support.",
          "text": "Expert guidance includes knowing what to examine next.\n\nWe want our recommendations to help you understand what we're considering and why. Sometimes that means giving a campaign more room to run while bringing a teammate into the review. That's the judgment we bring to the conversation."
        }
      ]
    },
    "email": {
      "subject_options": [
        "What we'd review while a campaign gathers data",
        "More time for the campaign, another look at the page"
      ],
      "preview": "How our team pairs evidence gathering with a useful next step.",
      "opening": "Hi {{contact.first_name}},\n\nA useful campaign review can leave you with two recommendations at once: give the campaign more time, and take a closer look at the landing page.",
      "body": "That combination came up in office hours with our Google Ads lead. She considered how much traffic there was to assess alongside what visitors were doing after the click, then recommended getting our Meta ads specialist's feedback on the page.\n\nFor venue owners investing in advertising to reach more couples, that distinction is useful. You want to know what to keep running and where another look could help guide your next decision.\n\nWe consider the opportunity a campaign has had to show us something. Days since launch and the amount of traffic available tell us different things, so we look at the traffic before recommending a change.\n\nVisitor behavior adds another perspective. In our Google Ads lead's work, visits lasting only a few seconds signal that something's off with the ads. When visitors spend time exploring but stop short of an inquiry, she looks more closely at what happens on the page.\n\nThose observations help her decide what to examine next. Bringing in our Meta ads specialist for feedback adds another way to review the experience between clicking an ad and completing the inquiry form.\n\nGathering more data and asking for that feedback can happen alongside each other. We want enough information to make a useful recommendation, and we want the owner to understand the reasoning behind it.",
      "ending": "That's part of the value of having experts in the conversation: a clearer view of what deserves time, what deserves attention and where another perspective can help.\n\nTalk soon,\nDaniela & Whitney"
    },
    "caption": {
      "hook": "A landing-page review can be useful while a campaign keeps gathering data.",
      "body": "When you're investing in advertising for your venue, you want a clear sense of what to keep running and what deserves attention. The timing of a campaign change is part of that conversation, and so is the experience people encounter after they click.\n\nIn office hours, our Google Ads lead considered those questions together. She wanted to give the campaign more breathing room and recommended asking our Meta ads specialist for feedback on the landing page.\n\nThe traffic helps us assess what the campaign has had an opportunity to do. Visitor behavior gives us another way to decide where a closer look could be useful.\n\nIn our Google Ads lead's work, people leaving after only a few seconds signals that something's off with the ads. People spending time exploring and then stopping short of an inquiry prompts a closer look at the page.\n\nThat reasoning helps shape a useful next step. The campaign can continue gathering data while the owner requests another perspective on the path to the inquiry form.\n\nFor a venue owner, expert support includes understanding why we're recommending more time and what we want to examine along the way.",
      "ending": "We want every recommendation to give you a clearer view of the next decision."
    },
    "stories": null
  },
